## 3) Sort with a custom Comparator

```java
public class CustomComparatorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Product[] products = {
            new Product("Laptop", 1200.00),
            new Product("Smartphone", 800.00),
            new Product("Headphones", 150.00),
            new Product("Tablet", 800.00)
        };

        Comparator<Product> productComparator = Comparator
                .comparingDouble(Product::price).reversed()
                .thenComparing(Product::name);

        Arrays.sort(products, productComparator);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(products));
    }
}
```

### Comparator styles

| Style | Example | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| Method reference / chained | `Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::price)` | Standard and clean sorting |
| Lambda expression | `(p1, p2) -> Double.compare(p2.price(), p1.price())` | Inline custom logic |
| Anonymous inner class | `new Comparator<Product>() { ... }` | Legacy or more complex custom logic |

